<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koh-i-Noor</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kohinoor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Koh-i-Noor, meaning 'Mountain of Light', is one of the most famous diamonds in the world, originating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> carried it off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a> after his invasion of Mughal India in 1739. It later passed through Afghan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> hands before being taken by the British, and it is now among the British Crown Jewels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koh-i-noor.jpg" alt="Koh-i-Noor" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: aiva. · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Replica_of_the_Koh-i-Noor_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1310 the Delhi Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> acquired a large diamond after subjugating the Kakatiya dynasty of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the 18th-century chronicler Khafi Khan identifies the stone as the Koh-i-Noor. The diamond first appears in the historical record in the late 15th century and later came into the possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. After Babur's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> defeated Ibrahim Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> in 1526, the family of Bikramjit, the ruler of Gwalior, presented Humayun with a diamond. Babur wrote that its value could feed the whole world for a day.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> the stone was set in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a>. Nader Shah took it with the throne from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1739 after defeating Muhammad Shah at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karnal">Battle of Karnal</a>. After Nader Shah's assassination in 1747 it passed to Ahmad Shah, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>; accounts differ on how. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-shuja-durrani">Shah Shuja Durrani</a> fled with it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, and in 1813 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> took it from him. Shah Shuja's memoirs state that Ranjit Singh extorted it by having his son tortured in front of him.</p><p>The Last Treaty of Lahore of 29 March 1849, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-sikh-war">Second Anglo-Sikh War</a>, annexed the remaining territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>. Its third article required the eleven-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duleep-singh">Maharaja Duleep Singh</a> to surrender the gem to the Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It was shown at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> of 1851, where its flawed appearance disappointed viewers. Prince Albert had it recut in 1852, which took 38 days and reduced it from 186 old carats to 105.6 carats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Since reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the diamond has been worn only by women of the royal family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> wore it as a brooch and circlet, and it was later set in the crowns of Queen Alexandra, Queen Mary in 1911 and Queen Elizabeth the Queen Mother in 1937. It is displayed in the Jewel House at the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. India asked for its return in 1947 and again in 1953, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1976, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> have also made claims, while the British government holds that it was acquired legally under the Last Treaty of Lahore. Wilkie Collins drew on its history for his novel The Moonstone of 1868.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koh-i-Noor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Peacock Throne and the <b>Koh-i-Noor</b> and Darya-i-Noor diamonds were among the prizes carried back to Persia, together with large numbers of artisans and craftsmen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Golconda was famous for its diamond mines, which produced the <b>Koh-i-Noor</b>, and for cotton textiles exported from Masulipatnam.</blockquote><a href="/state/golconda-sultanate">Golconda Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
